## Why This Is Asked
This tests cross-cloud module design, provider aliasing, and policy-driven governance at scale.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform multi-provider modules
- Provider aliasing and per-tenant scoping
- Open Policy Agent integration with terraform show -json plan
- CI gates and fail-fast

## Code Example
```javascript
// Module interface skeleton (Terraform-like)
module data_lake {
  source = ./modules/data_lake
  tenants = {
    t1 = { cloud = aws, region = us-east-1 }
    t2 = { cloud = gcp, region = us-central1 }
  }
  quotas = {
    t1 = { data_lake_size_gb = 100 }
    t2 = { data_lake_size_gb = 200 }
  }
}
```

```javascript
// Sample OPA policy skeleton
package terraform.policy
default allow = false
allow {
  input.resource_changes[_].change.actions[_] == create
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-cloud drift?
- How would you version and rollback policy changes?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?
